WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:12.560
Today we are going to see how NVIDIA’s new&nbsp;
AI can do 3 seemingly impossible things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.640
with just one elegant technique.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.200
For reference, here is a previous method that&nbsp;
can take a collection of photos like these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.800
and magically, create a video where we can fly&nbsp;
through these photos. This is what we call a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:37.760
NERF-based technique, and these are truly amazing.&nbsp;
Essentially, photos go in, and reality comes out.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:45.440
So, I know you are thinking, Károly, this looks&nbsp;
like science fiction. Can even this be topped?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:52.400
And the answer is yes. Yes it can!
Now, you see, this new technique can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:59.040
look at a small collection of photos, and, be&nbsp;
it people or cats, and it learns to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:05.840
continuous video of them. These look&nbsp;
fantastic. And remember, most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:14.080
information that you see here is synthetic.&nbsp;
Which means, it is created by the AI. So good!

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.520
But, wait, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.280
because there is a twist! It is often the&nbsp;
case for some techniques that they think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.800
in terms of photos. While other techniques&nbsp;
think in terms of volumes. And get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:40.080
this is a hybrid technique that thinks in terms of&nbsp;
both! Okay…so what does that mean? It means this!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:48.720
Yes, it also learned to now only generate these&nbsp;
photos, but also, the 3D geometry of these models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:56.240
at the same time. And this quality for the results&nbsp;
is truly something else. Look at how previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:03.200
techniques struggle with the same task. Wow. They&nbsp;
are completely different than the input model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:11.280
And you might think that of course they are not&nbsp;
so good. They are probably very old methods. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:17.440
not quite! Look, these are not some ancient&nbsp;
techniques, for instance, GIRAFFE is from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:26.000
the end of 2020 to the end of 2021 depending&nbsp;
on which variant they used. And, let’s see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:35.680
what the new method does on the same data…wow. My&nbsp;
goodness. Now that is something. Such improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.280
in so little time. The pace of progress in&nbsp;
AI research is nothing short of amazing.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:49.520
And not only that, but everything it produces is&nbsp;
multi-view consistent. This means that we don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:56.720
see a significant amount of flickering as we&nbsp;
rotate these models. There is a tiny bit on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.840
fur of the cats, but other than that, very little.&nbsp;
That is a super important usability feature.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:12.400
But wait, it does even more. Two, it can also&nbsp;
perform one of our favorites, super resolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:20.560
What is super resolution? Simple, a coarse,&nbsp;
pixelated image goes in, and what comes out?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.200
Of course, a beautiful, detailed image.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:35.760
And here comes number three. It projects these&nbsp;
images into a latent space. What does that mean?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.720
A latent space is a made-up place&nbsp;
where we are trying to organize data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.320
in a way that similar things are close&nbsp;
to each other. In our earlier work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.640
we were looking to generate hundreds of variants&nbsp;
of a material model to populate this scene. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:58.560
this latent space, we can concoct all of&nbsp;
these really cool digital material models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.920
A link to this work is available&nbsp;
in the video description.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:09.360
Now, let's see. Yes. When we take a walk in&nbsp;
the internal latent space of this technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:16.640
we can pick a starting point, a human face,&nbsp;
and generate these animations as this face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:23.840
morphs into other possible human faces. In short,&nbsp;
this can generate a variety of different people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:31.520
Very cool. I love it! Now, of course,&nbsp;
not even this technique is perfect, I see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.280
some flickering around the teeth, but otherwise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:43.200
this will be a fantastic tool for creating virtual&nbsp;
people. And remember, not only photos of virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.920
people, we get the 3D geometry for their&nbsp;
heads too. With this, we are one step closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:56.720
to democratizing the creation of virtual humans&nbsp;
in our virtual worlds. What a time to be alive!

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:03.920
And, if you have been holding on to your papers&nbsp;
so far, now, squeeze that paper! Because get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.840
you can do all of this in real time. And all&nbsp;
of these applications can be done with just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:18.080
elegant AI technique. Once again, scientists at&nbsp;
NVIDIA knocked it out of the park with this one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:26.720
Bravo. So, what about you? If all this can be&nbsp;
done in real time, what would you use this for?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.320
I’d love to know - please let&nbsp;
me know in the comments below.

